+**Your findings** come from the persona reviews (wave 2) running now on e800c43's build (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/cycle-beta7-dist). The lead will send more as reviewers confirm them; add them to your list.
+1. **BN-1 (High), false Location counts with a pay minimum and one place picked** (persona-beta-2-18-24).
+   - Google + Warsaw + "PLN 150,000" (about $40K+): the Location popover lists only Warsaw 60, Krakow 7, Wrocław 4, Dublin 2, London 1, and its search box disappears. Ticking Dublin then gives Dublin 139; Dublin alone with the same minimum is 137 roles.
+   - The same on other boards: Amazon + Toronto + $50K shows Vancouver 22 (really 178) and no Seattle at all (really 3,668); Apple + Cupertino + $100K shows Sunnyvale 32 (really 421).
+   - Cause (the reviewer's reading; confirm it): public/app.js derive() skips a posting that fails two filters. With a place picked, 'sal' is judged at that place (payHere), so a posting in any other place fails both 'l' and 'sal' and is never counted. That breaks the rule that a facet's counts ignore its own selection.
+   - Fix: for the 'l' counts, judge 'sal' at the counted place (or without the location selection), so each place's count is what ticking it alone would give. Check every facet's counts the same way (department, level, workplace, company where present) with every other filter, and say which you checked. The search box must not disappear.
+   - A unit test over derive() if it can be reached, and e2e: the count shown for a place equals the roles shown after picking it alone, on three boards.
+2. **BN-2 (Medium), a bare "$" median over a view in one foreign currency** (persona-beta-2-18-24).
+   - Amazon interns in Vancouver: the stats line says "$72K base pay median" above cards for CA$161K, CA$101K and CA$92K. Microsoft interns in Canada read "$34K", Google Canada "$86K", Google Warsaw "$75K" over PLN cards, and Google Dublin "$88K" over € cards. The map pin in Toronto reads "$72K · 5".
+   - MR-39 adds "(US)" or "(euro area)" only when two or more currencies are in view (basisShort needs st.basis), so a view in one foreign currency gets no label. Only the tooltip, which a phone never shows, says "in US dollars". A Canadian reads "$72K" as CA$72K.
+   - Fix: when every posting in view is in one currency, give the median in that currency (CA$101K), as the cards show it; when the view mixes currencies, keep MR-39's rule and say the basis. Never a bare "$" for a converted figure. The stats line, Insights' tiles, the map's pin labels and the chart caption must agree.
+   - Owners for this item: public/app.js (the stats line and stats()' basis), public/features/insights.js (the tiles), and the label text only in the map and chart files. fix-ui-6, running beside you, owns the map's fit; don't change it.
